        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are walking pads safe for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They can be, with the right machine and habits. Choose one with long handrails, a slow start speed and a safety key or stop button, set it up with clear space behind, and always clip on the key. Anyone with balance problems, a heart condition or a fall history should check with their doctor first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best speed for a senior on a walking machine?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start at the slowest speed, often 0.3 to 0.6 mph on senior machines, and build up gradually. Many older walkers settle at about 1.5 to 2.5 mph. The right pace is one where you can talk comfortably.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need handrails on a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most older walkers, yes. Long side rails give support throughout the walk, including stepping on and off. Hold them lightly for balance rather than gripping and leaning back.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight capacity should I choose?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Choose a machine rated at least 50 lb above the heaviest person who will use it. A higher rating usually means a sturdier frame and deck, which feel steadier underfoot.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between a walking pad and a walking machine?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              "Walking machine" is used loosely for any treadmill aimed at walking. A walking pad is a low, slim, usually console-free treadmill. Senior walking machines are typically walking pads with handrails and a simple console added.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a recumbent bike safer than a walking pad for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For people with poor balance, usually yes, because there is no moving belt to fall from. A walking machine with long rails suits steadier walkers who want weight-bearing exercise.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

